{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on bffe447a: both bridges confirmed, in C3 after NF-5 lands.\n\n1. The browser request layer maps the outcome `unsent` to the status\n   `unknown`, in settledOfSent and in settledOf. A record in the phase\n   `refused` whose decided outcome is `unsent` gives `unknown`. It\n   never gives `refused` with the reason `refused`. This follows the\n   client note, section 5.5: a reader with no replica reads such a\n   record as it reads a folded record, and this layer reads a folded\n   record as `unknown`.\n2. The layer adds no status and no page copy. No page says that the\n   service refused, and no page says that nothing was sent.\n3. readStoredOutcome admits the `unsent` variant only: a signing index\n   that is a nonnegative safe integer, and a valid Head with seq and\n   hash. A mixed kind, a missing or null signing and a malformed Head\n   stay unreadable. No layout version and no migration change. Stored\n   bytes grant no authority.\n4. A later fold of the entry moves the record to `accepted` (the late\n   seal of section 5.5), and the layer then gives `recorded`.\n\nNamed cases, one test each: the store round trip of a record that\n`absent` ended. Each malformed variant of item 3 is unreadable. The\nrecord gives `unknown` through settledOf and through settledOfSent.\nThe same record gives `recorded` after `folded`. Counter shows no\nrefusal copy for the record. One control: without the settledOf\nbranch, the record gives `refused`, and its case fails.\n\nThe stronger fact, a proved absence with its head, reaches a page in\nC4 and E3 as the cause `{ by: \"client\", reason: \"unsent\" }`. The\nreview after E3 takes the field that says whether a request can still\nrecord. Run the whole browser-request, store and custody owners and\nthe whole typecheck. NF-5 at b8d58825 stays frozen.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}